WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:03.325
Easy Science. Why Is the Sea Salty?

00:00:03.325 --> 00:00:08.159
If you have ever swum in the sea, you know how salty the water is.

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:11.726
But the water in most rivers and lakes is not salty.

00:00:11.726 --> 00:00:13.794
Where does the salt come from?

00:00:13.794 --> 00:00:16.515
Most of it comes from rocks on the land.

00:00:16.515 --> 00:00:18.920
Rain water is not quite pure.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.330
It can slowly break down rock.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:27.023
Over a very long time, rain takes tiny amounts of salt out of the rocks.

00:00:27.023 --> 00:00:32.441
Small streams carry this salt to rivers, and rivers carry it to the sea.

00:00:32.441 --> 00:00:35.482
The amount of salt in a river is very small.

00:00:35.482 --> 00:00:37.139
You cannot taste it.

00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:41.837
But rivers have been running into the sea for millions and millions of years.

00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:43.833
And the salt does not leave.

00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:49.712
When the sun warms the sea, water goes up into the air and later falls as rain.

00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:51.795
But only the water goes up.

00:00:51.795 --> 00:00:54.229
The salt stays behind in the sea.

00:00:54.229 --> 00:00:58.241
So, little by little, the sea has become salty.

00:00:58.241 --> 00:01:02.983
Some salt also comes from hot places deep at the bottom of the sea.

00:01:02.983 --> 00:01:08.243
Today there is about thirty-five grams of salt in every liter of sea water.

00:01:08.243 --> 00:01:12.466
That is about two big spoons of salt in a bottle of water.
